Inheritance in programming.

Inheritance is a fundamental concept in object-oriented programming (OOP) that allows one class to create a new class based on another existing class. The new class, called "child", inherits the properties and methods of the original class, called "parent". In this way, the child can use the attributes and behaviors of the parent without having to reimplement them.

Inheritance allows the use of a code reuse mechanism, as if a new class needs to perform some operations similar to those performed by another class, it is not necessary to write new code. Instead, the new class can inherit the methods and properties needed by the parent.

Inheritance also allows the use of a more organized programming model, as it allows related classes to be connected to each other through the "parent-child" relationship.

Here is an example in C++ to illustrate inheritance:
```cpp
// Parent class (base)
class Animal {
  public:
    void eat() { std::cout << "The animal eats." << std::endl; }
};

// Child class that inherits from Parent
class Dog : public Animal {
  public:
    void bark() { std::cout << "The dog barks." << std::endl; }
};

int main() {
  Dog myDog;
  myDog.eat(); // The animal eats.
  myDog.bark(); // The dog barks.

  return 0;
}
```
In this example, the class `Dog` inherits the properties and methods of the class `Animal`, and in addition adds its own method `bark()`. Inheritance allows the class `Dog` to use the attributes and behaviors of the parent without having to reimplement them.

[PROGRAMACION]: La palabra clave `virtual` en C++ se utiliza para indicar que una función puede ser sobrecargada por clases derivadas. Esto permite la polimorfismo, es decir, la capacidad de un objeto tener varios comportamientos dependiendo del tipo real al que pertenece.

En resumen, se usa `virtual` para:

1. Permitir la sobrescritura de métodos en clases derivadas.
2. Habilitar el llamado dinámico a funciones virtuales, es decir, determinar qué función llamar en tiempo de ejecución dependiendo del tipo real del objeto.

Un ejemplo sencillo:
```cpp
class Base {
public:
    virtual void metodo() { std::cout << "Metodo base"; }
};

class Derivada : public Base {
public:
    void metodo() override { std::cout << "Metodo derivado"; }
};

int main() {
    Base* ptr = new Derivada();
    ptr->metodo(); // Llamando a la función virtual mediante un puntero
}
```
En este ejemplo, la clase `Derivada` sobrescribe el método `metodo()` de la clase base. Al llamar a `metodo()` desde un objeto de tipo `Base*`, se usa el método sobrecargado en la clase `Derivada`. Si no usáramos `virtual`, solo se llamaría al método original de la clase `Base`.

